### Refactoring

**You own the contract. Structure changes; behavior does not.** Split discovered features or bugs into separately scoped work.

Read the [sign-off contract](../references/sign-off.md) before preparation or implementation.

1. Run **how** over the affected subsystem and specify the behavior pin: characterization test, snapshot or equivalence harness. Capture a safe existing baseline or scratch diagnostic result before approval, but do not write the shipping pin yet. Type checking and lint are not a behavior pin.
2. Name the structure and organizing shape the code needs. State target modules/types/call graph and what branches or invalid states it removes. Boring local code stays when already clear. Run **architect** for design only when crossing a function boundary. Large refactors still match this method; use [Multi-phase plan](multi-phase-plan.md) and [Orchestrate](orchestrate.md) when execution needs dependent units.
3. Present one proposal covering the pin, target shape, caller migration, actual paths, equivalence checks, exclusions and action limits. Stop unless matching approval or an explicit grant covers implementation. The coordinator owns approval once.
4. Within authority, delegate to `implementer` or `implementer-hard` carrying actual approval context or evidence, scope, exclusions and stop conditions. The first product change writes and proves the shipping behavior pin before any structural move. Preserve its actual receipt/evidence. For coordinated moves use [run accounting](../references/run-contract.md).
5. Subtract dead weight, then move in small behavior-preserving slices, keeping the pin green. Migrate all callers and delete old APIs in the same wave where feasible, without adding unrequested compatibility paths. Spot-check strings, prose and back-references as well as symbol usages. No automatic rebases, shared-history rewrites or unrelated cleanup.
6. Prove equivalence on the real artifact and matching surface. Preserve old/new output comparisons and pin evidence, not a "compiles" claim. Keep the reshape only when it reduces reader load without changing behavior. Revert only owned unchanged edits; concurrent/unrelated changes stay intact.
7. Run **review-panel**, correct covered findings, and re-prove equivalence. Prepare verified ordered commits and [Opening a PR](opening-a-pr.md) only within history/delivery limits.

**Reply:** structure changed, pin, equivalence proof, reader-load tradeoff, migrated/deleted paths, rejected work, review and actual delivery. No new behavior.
